WHY NO KERNEL CAN DO MUCH BETTER AT M=32
  minimum bytes that must move     4.72 MB  (B once, A per column block)
  arithmetic intensity             21.3 FLOP/byte
  machine balance (peak / BW)      82.4 FLOP/byte
  => hard ceiling at M=32          25.9% of peak, whatever the code does
  this kernel reaches               5.5% of peak =  21.1% of that ceiling

  The M=32 shape is byte-bound: 21 FLOP/byte cannot buy 82 of
machine. Raising M is the only thing that moves this ratio; tiling, registers and split-K cannot.

one expert at M=32: 302 MFLOP
  arithmetic at DERIVED peak   12.166 us
  arithmetic in this kernel      222.6 us  (18x off peak)
  transfer over PCIe (11.6 GB/s)  212.6 us

WHAT BIT-WIDTH BUYS, at the MEASURED 11.6 GB/s PCIe
Every row is one full 128-expert sweep -- the worst case, where all
32 tokens route to disjoint experts. Quality is NOT modelled here;
only bytes are. An actual W2/W3 design has to beat the 81.5 dB SNR
penalty that killed the int4 activation path (gemm_w4.hip).
   format   B/weight   expert MB   128 experts   vs peak arithmetic
   W4 g128    0.5234       2.47      27.22 ms        17.5x  (transfer-bound)
   W3 g128    0.3984       1.88      20.72 ms        13.3x  (transfer-bound)
   W2 g128    0.2734       1.29      14.22 ms         9.1x  (transfer-bound)

  To become compute-bound, PCIe would have to reach 203.0 GB/s at W4.
  No shipping interconnect is close, so the crossover cannot be moved
  by tuning -- only by moving fewer bytes.
  Halving to W2 only buys 1.9x, because the arithmetic side is already
  near free. That is the honest shape of this problem: it is a byte-
  movement problem wearing an inference engine's clothes.

